# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf = true` in its system config
# (`C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig`). This working copy lives on a Windows
# drive and is used from WSL, where git has `core.autocrlf` unset. Any
# Windows-side git operation therefore checks tracked text files out as CRLF,
# and WSL git then reports hundreds of unchanged files as modified.
#
# A path attribute beats `core.autocrlf`, so `eol=lf` makes Windows git write
# LF too. Measured 2026-10-09: `git.exe reset --hard` in a clone without this
# file produced 319 CRLF files and 303 dirty files; in a clone with it, zero.
#
# `text=auto` also detects a binary file and leaves its bytes alone.
#
# Limit: this does not hide a CRLF working file from `git status`. If the churn
# returns while `git check-attr text eol -- <file>` still reports `auto` and
# `lf`, some other Windows tool wrote the CRLF, and git is not the cause.
* text=auto eol=lf
